      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "The aim of this study was to compare the surgical outcomes of endoscopic thyroidectomy (ET) and conventional open thyroidectomy (COT) for benign thyroid nodules."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Between March 2001 and November 2014, 224 patients underwent ET via the breast approach and 218 patients underwent COT. Clinicopathological characteristics and surgical outcomes were retrospectively compared."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "The operation time was significantly longer in the ET group than in the COT group (P\u200a=\u200a0.000). However, the ET group had less intraoperative blood loss (P\u200a=\u200a0.000), less amount of drainage (P\u200a=\u200a0.000), and shorter duration of drainage (P\u200a=\u200a0.000). The cosmetic satisfaction was more excellent in the ET group than in the COT group (P\u200a=\u200a0.000)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "ET via the breast approach is a safe and effective procedure with excellent cosmetic results for patients with benign thyroid nodules."
        }
      ]},
